# Dashboard YAML Structure

Dashboards are defined as YAML files in `dashboards/<root>/`. Each root has a `+layout.yaml` for root metadata and one or more `NN-name.yaml` dashboard files. Dashboards can also be **directory-based** — a `NN-name/` directory with `_tab.yaml` (preferred) or `_dashboard.yaml` (deprecated, validator emits warning) and tab files.

## Canonical hierarchy

`root -> dashboard -> tab -> [sections -> widgets]`

## Authoring modes (strict)

- **Flat mode**: one tab file contains everything (`title`, `datasources`, `sections`, etc.).
- **Directory mode**: strict split by responsibility:
  - dashboard `_tab.yaml` = meta only (id/title/description/icon/ai/appearance/freshness)
  - tab-directory `_tab.yaml` = tab meta (id/title/icon/filters)
  - `_shared/*.yaml` = shared datasources/mappings/filters
  - `NN-*.yaml` = tab-content files with `sections: [...]` (and optional file-local `datasources`)
- Do not mix these semantics: in directory mode, `_tab.yaml` must not contain `datasources` or `sections`.

## Top-level fields

```yaml
id: my_dashboard           # Required. Unique identifier (snake_case)
title: My Dashboard        # Required. Display title
icon: BarChart3            # Optional. Lucide icon name
description: Description   # Optional. Shown in sidebar

ai:                        # Optional. AI assistant config
  roles:                     # Optional. Audience roles for this dashboard
    preferred: buyer         # Primary role (key from _context.yaml ai.roles)
    extra: [owner, financier] # Additional roles
  dataset:
    name: Dataset Name
    description: What data is in this dashboard
    language: en             # Optional. Default AI response language (en | ru)
    date_column: date        # Optional. Primary date column for profiling/expand
    metrics: {}              # Optional. Named metrics with formulas
    dimensions: []           # Optional. Dimension column names
    profiling:               # Optional. Data profiling config
      enabled: true
    business:                # Optional. Business context
      rules: []
  welcome: "Ask about data"  # Optional. Welcome message (English)
  welcome_ru: "Спросите"     # Optional. Welcome message (Russian)
  examples:                  # Optional. Example questions (English)
    - "Total spend?"
  examples_ru:               # Optional. Example questions (Russian)
    - "Общие расходы?"

mappings: {}               # Optional. Value mapping dictionaries (see dashboard-mappings rule)
datasources: {}            # Required. Named datasources (see dashboard-datasources rule)
filters: []                # Optional. Dashboard-level filters (see dashboard-filters rule)
freshness: ...             # Optional. Data freshness config (see dashboard-datasources rule)
tabs: []                   # Optional. Tabbed layout
sections: []               # Required. Array of sections with widgets
```

## Sections

Sections group widgets. Each section renders a 12-column grid.

```yaml
sections:
  - id: overview                      # Required. Unique within dashboard
    title: Overview                   # Optional. Shows section header with collapse
    description: Section description  # Optional
    layout: vertical                  # Optional. vertical (default) or horizontal
    columns: 2                        # Optional. For horizontal layout
    gap: 4                            # Optional. Gap between widgets in px
    collapsed: false                  # Optional. Start collapsed
    shareable: true                   # Optional. Show share link button (default: true when title is set)
    filters: []                       # Optional. Section-level filters
    widgets: []                       # Required. Array of widgets
    ai_hint: "..."                    # Optional. AI context hint (string or string[])
```

### Horizontal layout

Use `layout: horizontal` with `columns: N` to place widgets side by side in equal-width columns:

```yaml
- id: side-by-side
  title: Side by Side Charts
  layout: horizontal
  columns: 2
  widgets:
    - id: chart-left
      type: chart
      # ...
    - id: chart-right
      type: chart
      # ...
```

## Widget base fields (shared by all widget types)

```yaml
- id: my_widget            # Required. Unique within section
  type: rich_table         # Required. One of: rich_table, markdown, chart
  col_span: 12             # Optional. Grid columns (1-12, default 12)
  row_span: 1              # Optional. Grid rows
  filters:                 # Optional. Control which dashboard/section filters apply
    include: [date_range]  # Only these filters
    exclude: [media_source] # All except these
  inline_filters: []       # Optional. Widget-scoped filter definitions
  shareable: true          # Optional. Show share link button (default: true when widget has a title)
  ai_hint: "..."           # Optional. AI context hint (string or string[])
```

## Tabs

Tabs allow splitting a dashboard into tabbed views. Each tab has its own sections and optional filters:

```yaml
tabs:
  - id: overview
    title: Overview
    filters: []            # Optional. Tab-level filters
    sections: []           # Sections within this tab
  - id: details
    title: Details
    sections: []
```

When `tabs` is present, top-level `sections` still exist (rendered above tabs).

## Root layout (+layout.yaml)

```yaml
name: Product                           # Required. Root display name
icon: TrendingUp                        # Optional. Lucide icon
description: Product dashboards         # Optional
order: 1                                # Optional. Sort order in sidebar

settings:
  default_dashboard: 01-marketing       # Optional. Default dashboard to open

links:                                  # Optional. External links in sidebar
  - title: Tableau
    url: https://example.com
    icon: /icons/tableau.png            # Optional
    external: true                      # Optional
```

## Root-level shared definitions

A root can have a `_shared/` directory (next to `+layout.yaml`) with datasources, filters, and mappings inherited by **all dashboards** in that root:

```text
dashboards/
  <root>/
    +layout.yaml                         # UI metadata (unchanged)
    _shared/                             # Root-level shared definitions
      _datasources.yaml                  # Shared datasources for all dashboards
      _filters.yaml                      # Shared filters for all dashboards
      _mappings.yaml                     # Shared mappings for all dashboards
    01-dashboard-a/                      # Inherits root _shared/
    02-dashboard-b.yaml                  # Inherits root _shared/
```

### Merge hierarchy (3 tiers)

```text
Root _shared/         (base layer)
  ↓
Dashboard _shared/    (override / extend)
  ↓
Tab-level             (extend with projections only)
```

- **Datasources & mappings**: extend — root is base, dashboard wins on key conflict
- **Filters**: override — if dashboard defines ANY filters (inline or via `_shared/_filters.yaml`), root filters are completely replaced; otherwise root filters are inherited

All three files are optional. If `_shared/` does not exist at root level, nothing is inherited.

## Global AI context (`_context.yaml`)

A `_context.yaml` file can be defined inside each root (`dashboards/<root>/_context.yaml`) and contributes root-scoped AI assistant behaviour.
When multiple roots are configured, context is loaded from all roots in configured order.

When authoring visual widgets, align with the shared A7S Skipper design system:

- Prefer named palettes `default`, `ocean`, `sunset` for chart series.
- Prefer design-system tokens and semantic roles over ad-hoc hex colors.
- Keep status colors semantic; do not reuse them as arbitrary chart palette replacements.
- If MCP is available, inspect `skipper://design-system` and `skipper://design-system/palettes`.

RBAC v2 notes for dashboard structure:

- Dashboard paths are the canonical RBAC resource namespace in A7S Skipper.
- Access is granted through org-level grants, not through YAML-local role declarations or team globs.
- Keep root/dashboard/tab IDs stable once shared, because renaming paths changes the resource names grants match against.

```yaml
ai:
  roles:                        # Audience role definitions (referenced by dashboards)
    buyer:
      title: "User Acquisition Manager"
      focus: ["CPI", "ROAS", "spend efficiency"]
      instructions: "Focus on acquisition costs and budget optimization."
    owner:
      title: "Business Owner / CEO"
      focus: ["revenue", "profit", "growth"]
      instructions: "High-level overview, strategic metrics."
      email: "owner@company.com"                # Optional. Contact shown in header Help popover
      slack_url: "https://yourworkspace.slack.com/..."  # Optional. Contact link in header Help popover

  style:                        # Response style bullets injected into AI system prompt
    - "Answer like an analyst: direct, specific numbers, bold key figures."
    - 'Use explicit dates (e.g. "2024-03-01"), not "today".'

  rules:                        # Generic rule categories (any key allowed)
    business:
      hint: "Core business definitions and constraints"
      items:
        - "ROAS below 3.0 is considered poor"
    marketing:
      hint: "Acquisition and advertising metrics"
      items:
        - "CPI = spend / installs"
      glossary:
        CPI: "Cost Per Install — total ad spend divided by number of installs"
        ROAS: "Return On Ad Spend — revenue divided by ad spend"
    data:
      hint: "Data quality, freshness, granularity"
      items:
        - "Today's data may be incomplete — ad spend lags by 3-4 hours."
    dates:
      hint: "Date ranges and period handling"
      items:
        - "Default analysis period is last 30 days."
```

### Roles

Role definitions have:

- `title` (optional) — human-readable role name
- `focus` (optional) — array of key metrics/areas this role cares about
- `instructions` (optional) — tone and focus guidance for the AI

Dashboards reference roles by key via `ai.roles.preferred` and `ai.roles.extra`. The AI renders an `<audience>` section with primary and secondary roles, adjusting focus and tone accordingly.

### Rules

Each rule category has:

- `hint` (optional) — context for the AI about what this category covers
- `items` — array of rule strings
- `glossary` (optional) — `Record<string, string>` of term definitions; rendered as `<glossary><term name="...">...</term></glossary>` inside the category

Category keys are arbitrary — add any domain-specific categories you need (e.g. `financial`, `product`, `compliance`).

### Merge with per-dashboard config

- `style` — replaces hardcoded defaults entirely when present
- `roles` — definitions are global; dashboards select which roles apply
- `rules` — all categories are rendered as `<rules category="...">` in the system prompt
- Per-dashboard `ai.dataset.business.rules` are added as a separate `category="dashboard"` block

If the file is missing or empty, built-in style defaults are used and no rules/roles are injected.

### Root owner contacts (navigation metadata)

You can optionally define navigation roots and owner contacts in each root `_context.yaml`:

```yaml
navigation:
  roots:
    - id: product
      name: Product
      icon: LayoutDashboard
      folders: [samples, platform]
      owner:
        name: "Jane Owner"
        team: "Product Analytics"
        email: "jane@company.com"
        slack_url: "https://yourworkspace.slack.com/team/..."
```

- `owner` is optional.
- When present, owner info is shown in the dashboard header Help popover together with changelog links.
- `owner.team` is optional and shown in the Help popover when provided.
- `slack_url` or `slackUrl` are both accepted in navigation owner object.

### Canonical dashboard URLs

- Dashboard URL: `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>`
- Tab URL: `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>/<tab>`
- `/dashboards/<root>/<dashboard>` redirects to default or first tab.
- Any other non-canonical dashboard URL pattern returns `404`.
- `root` is derived from configured dashboard roots order (`--root`/`--roots` or `A7S_ROOTS_DIR`).

## Root product changelog (`_changelog.yaml`)

Each root can have a separate non-technical product changelog:

- Root path: `dashboards/<root>/_changelog.yaml`

```yaml
entries:
  - date: "2026-03-05"
    title: "New KPI cards"
    summary: "Added simplified KPI cards for business users."
```

- Use product/business language, not engineering internals.
- This changelog is shown in the header Help popover.
- Technical changelog remains on `/docs/changelog`.

### MCP root resources

- `skipper://roots` — list of configured roots.
- `skipper://roots/<root>/changelog` — root product changelog.
- `skipper://query-results/{id}` — ephemeral full query result as CSV (1-hour TTL, lazy-evaluated).
- `update_root_changelog` MCP tool updates `dashboards/<root>/_changelog.yaml`.

### Multi-root input

**Singular flags** (direct root, repeatable):
`--root`, `--workspace`, `--board`
Example: `--root dashboards/product --root dashboards/revenue`

**Plural flags** (parent dir, auto-scan subdirectories):
`--roots`, `--workspaces`, `--boards`
Example: `--roots ./dashboards` scans `product/`, `revenue/`, etc.

**Env var** (colon-separated):

- `A7S_ROOTS_DIR` — paths with smart resolution (YAML at top level = direct root, otherwise auto-scan children)

Roots order defines root order in sidebar/navigation.

## Directory-based dashboards

A dashboard can be a directory instead of a single file. The loader auto-detects the format:

| Entry                  | Mode                                     |
|------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `NN-name.yaml` (file)  | Flat - single file, current behavior     |
| `NN-name/` (directory) | Directory - assembled from files inside  |

### Directory structure

```text
NN-name/
  _tab.yaml                # Dashboard meta (id, title, description, icon, ai, appearance, freshness)
  # _dashboard.yaml        # Deprecated meta alias (validator emits deprecation warning)
  _shared/                 # Optional shared definitions
    _datasources.yaml      # Shared datasources (extend)
    _mappings.yaml         # Shared mappings (extend)
    _filters.yaml          # Replaces dashboard-level filters entirely
  00-overview.yaml         # Simple tab (title, sections)
  10-revenue/              # Tab directory (optional)
    _tab.yaml              # Tab meta (id/title/icon/filters)
    00-kpis.yaml           # Tab-content file (must contain sections: [...])
    10-charts.yaml         # Tab-content file (must contain sections: [...])
  20-settings.yaml         # Simple tab
```

### Naming rules

- `NN-` prefix determines order (step of 10 for insertion room)
- `_` prefix = meta/config, never content
- `+layout.yaml` = root-level only, not inside dashboard dirs
- Slug derived by stripping numeric prefix: `10-revenue` → `revenue`
- URL segments for `root`, `dashboard`, and `tab` must be kebab-case: `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`

### `_shared/` merge semantics

- `_datasources.yaml` and `_mappings.yaml`: **extend** — shared is base, tab-content inline can extend/override on key conflict
- `_filters.yaml`: **override** — replaces dashboard-level filters entirely

### Tab-level datasource extension

Tabs can extend existing datasources by adding projections:

```yaml
# In a tab-content file (NN-*.yaml), not in _tab.yaml
datasources:
  orders_main:               # existing datasource name
    projections:
      revenue_by_month:
        sql: |
          SELECT ... FROM {{source:orders_main}}
```

### Strict tab-content contract

- Standalone section files (`id + widgets` at top level) are not supported.
- Any non-meta tab content file must be a tab file with top-level `sections: [...]`.
- In tab directories, `_tab.yaml` is tab meta; sibling `NN-*.yaml` files are tab-content fragments and must each contain `sections`.
- In tab directories, `_tab.yaml` cannot contain `datasources` or `sections`.
- `_dashboard.yaml` is deprecated and triggers a warning; migrate to `_tab.yaml` + `_shared/_datasources.yaml` + `_shared/_filters.yaml`.

### CLI: split a flat dashboard

```bash
bun run bin/cli.mjs --split-dashboard path/to/dashboard.yaml
```

## AI Hints (`ai_hint`)

Optional field available at 6 levels: datasource, projection, projection column, section, widget, and widget column (rich_table). Provides operational context for the AI assistant — not displayed in UI, only injected into the AI system prompt.

Accepts a string or an array of strings:

```yaml
ai_hint: "Pre-aggregated by day"

ai_hint:
  - "Pre-aggregated by day"
  - "Contains PII in user_email"
```

Use `ai_hint` for advice on _how to use_ the data (vs. `description` which says _what it is_).

Column-level `description` acts as `ai_hint` fallback — when a rich_table column has `description` but no explicit `ai_hint`, the description text is used as the AI hint. Write `description` for what the data _is_; write `ai_hint` for how to _use_ it.

## Layout system

### Prefer section-level layout over widget-level `col_span`

Use `layout: horizontal` with `columns: N` on sections to arrange widgets side by side. This is the **preferred** approach for multi-column layouts — it produces equal-width columns, handles responsive behavior, and keeps widget definitions clean.

```yaml
sections:
  - id: kpi-cards
    layout: horizontal
    columns: 3
    widgets:
      - id: revenue-card
        type: markdown
        # no col_span needed — section divides space equally
      - id: costs-card
        type: markdown
      - id: roi-card
        type: markdown

  - id: charts-row
    layout: horizontal
    columns: 2
    widgets:
      - id: trend-chart
        type: chart
      - id: breakdown-chart
        type: chart
```

### When to use `col_span` / `row_span` instead

Reserve `col_span` and `row_span` for cases that section layout cannot express:

| Use case | Approach |
|----------|----------|
| Side-by-side widgets (equal width) | `layout: horizontal` + `columns: N` |
| Side-by-side widgets (unequal width) | `col_span` on individual widgets |
| Wide table with horizontal scroll | `col_span: 15` (values above 12) |
| Multi-track charts needing more height | `row_span: 2` or higher |
| Full-width widget (default) | Neither — `col_span: 12` is the default |

### Grid system reference

- Default grid: 12 columns
- `col_span: 6` = half width, `col_span: 12` = full width (default)
- Values above 12 (e.g. `col_span: 15`) are allowed for wide tables with horizontal scroll
- `row_span` controls height in grid rows
